        "knowledge_point_id": "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7",
        "knowledge_point": "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse",
        "problem": "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes.",
        "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-6$ and $k=-4,$ so the center is located at $(-6,-4).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=5$ and ${b}^{2}=9.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=5,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=9,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=3,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3=6,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=2\\sqrt{5}.$",
