      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-9455",
        "knowledge_point_id": "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137",
        "knowledge_point": "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse",
        "problem": "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis.",
        "worked_solution": "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{9}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-1 & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(1,0).$",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices_complete": true,
            "choices": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "value": "(-1,0)",
